Apple has long rejected the idea of a touchscreen Mac, so moving ahead with one was always going to be a major shift in the company's thinking. In 2010, Steve Jobs argued that "touch surfaces don't want to be vertical," citing the arm fatigue that comes from repeatedly reaching up to a screen.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/oled-macbook-pro-touch.jpg" alt="" width="1653" height="930" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1074103" /><div class="center-wrap"><em>Some would call this sacrilege...</em></div><br/>
More than a decade later, in 2021, Apple's then-hardware engineering chief John Ternus remarked that the Mac was made exclusively for indirect input and that Apple saw no compelling reason to change its approach.<br/>
<br/>
"We make the world's best touch computer on an iPad. It's totally optimized for that. And the Mac is totally optimized for indirect input. We haven't really felt a reason to change that," Ternus said in an <a href="https://www.macrumors.com/2021/10/29/apple-face-id-touchscreens-macs/">interview</a> with Joanna Stern.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>Touch Comes to the Mac</h2><br/>
Fast forward to 2026, and Apple has had a change of heart. The new MacBook Pro with PROLED XDR is the first Mac to adopt touchscreen technology. Many younger people who grew up with smartphones and tablets expect to interact with their computers the same way they do with their mobile devices, and that expectation appears to have twisted Apple's arm (no pun intended).<br/>
<br/>
Of course, just because the new MacBook Pro models have a touchscreen doesn't mean you have to use it. If you forget it's even there, you can turn off the function and pretend it doesn't exist. If you use Sidecar with an iPad on another Mac, you can turn off touch in the same way.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>Turn Off Touch on MacBook Pro and Sidecar</h2><br/>
The steps are the same whether you want to disable the MacBook Pro's touchscreen or touch input in Sidecar:<br/>
<br/>
<ol><li>Click the Apple logo in the top-left corner of the menu bar and choose <strong>System Settings...</strong>.</li><br/>
<li>Select <strong>Accessibility</strong> in the sidebar.</li><br/>
<li>Under "Physical and Motor," click <strong>Touch</strong>.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/turn-off-touch-oled-macbook-pro2.jpg" alt="settings" width="1492" height="1069" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076731" /><br/>
<li>Toggle on the switch next to <strong>Ignore Touch Gestures</strong>.<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/turn-off-touch-oled-macbook-pro1.jpg" alt="settings" width="1495" height="876" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076733" /><br/>
</ol>That's all there is to it. Your Mac will now ignore touch input, and since the setting covers Sidecar too, an iPad you use as a second display will ignore touches as well. If you ever want touch back, return to the same screen and toggle off <strong>Ignore Touch Gestures</strong>.<br/>
<br/>
<h2>Touch Friendly, Not Touch First</h2><br/>
If some gestures prove useful but others keep being triggered by accident, you can adjust them individually instead of disabling touch altogether. In System Settings, select <strong>Touch Display</strong> in the sidebar, where each gesture can be switched off, and Swipe between spaces, Mission Control, and App Exposé also have pop-up menus for changing how the gesture is performed. <br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/11/touch-display-macos-sidecar-proled.jpg" alt="settings" width="1572" height="1344" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1076743" /><br/>
Don't overlook the fact that the Accessibility ➝ Touch screen (where "Ignore Touch Gestures" lives) offers further customization options. Touch Accommodations changes how the screen responds to taps, swipes, and touch-and-hold input. AssistiveTouch can also be found on this settings menu if you want alternative ways to control the screen, and the <strong>Accessibility ➝ Zoom</strong> menu includes an option to zoom using the touch display or Sidecar.<br/>
<br/>
<img src="https://images.macrumors.com/article-new/2026/10/macbook-pro-proled-touch.jpg" alt="" width="2260" height="1271" class="aligncenter size-full wp-image-1074117" /><br/>
